<DocSection Title="What data-level access controls">
    <p>
        TypeAuth on a controller answers <i>whether</i> a caller may use an endpoint.
        Data-level access answers <i>which rows</i> that caller may read, create,
        change, or delete. You normally need both: the controller checks the
        operation, then the repository applies the row policy.
    </p>

    <p>
        The v2 policy is declared once and drives both paths. It adds an
        <code>IQueryable</code> predicate for lists and finds, and evaluates the
        same dimensions against an entity for inserts, edits, and deletes.
    </p>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Note">
        These examples are compile-checked against the published framework packages.
        The query and row behavior is also verified through a scoped-token SQL
        Server lifecycle test covering list, find, insert, edit, and delete.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Opt the host into data-level access">
    <p>
        Data-level access is an explicit host choice. Add this once in the API
        or Functions host during service registration:
    </p>

    <DocSnippet Name="DataLevelAccessHostOptIn" Language="cs" />

    <p>
        This single call installs the scoped v2 policy engine and the standard
        seven-marker profile. Calling <code>AddShiftEntityWeb</code> alone does
        neither. The explicit opt-in prevents a package upgrade from silently
        changing authorization for every existing entity.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Use the standard marker profile">
    <p>
        After the host opts in, the framework recognizes these marker interfaces:
        <code>IEntityHasCountry</code>, <code>IEntityHasRegion</code>,
        <code>IEntityHasCompany</code>, <code>IEntityHasCompanyBranch</code>,
        <code>IEntityHasBrand</code>, <code>IEntityHasCity</code>, and
        <code>IEntityHasTeam</code>. Each marker contributes its standard
        ShiftIdentity data-level action, key column, and hash-ID type. It also
        contributes the matching self claim where that dimension has one; Brand
        has no self claim, while Team can use all of the caller's Team claims.
    </p>

    <DocSnippet Name="DataLevelAccessStandardMarker" Language="cs" />

    <p>
        This entity receives the standard Companies dimension automatically.
        Existing <code>DefaultDataLevelAccessOptions</code> disable flags are
        honored when the profile is built. Several marker dimensions are combined
        with AND: a row must satisfy every enabled dimension.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Override one standard dimension with OR keys">
    <p>
        Sometimes one logical dimension is stored in more than one column. The
        example below allows a company to reach a shipment through either its
        owner company or its intermediary company:
    </p>

    <DocSnippet Name="DataLevelAccessSameActionOverride" Language="cs" />

    <p>
        <code>Keys</code> means OR within this dimension. The row matches when
        either column is in the caller's accessible Companies set. Because the
        explicit declaration uses the same Companies action as the standard
        marker, it <b>replaces</b> the standard single-column Company dimension.
        It is not appended with AND. Standard dimensions for other marker actions
        remain automatic.
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><code>HashId&lt;CompanyDTO&gt;()</code> decodes the TypeAuth item IDs with the same Company hash-ID key used by ShiftIdentity.</li>
        <li><code>Self(...)</code> lets a TypeAuth self-reference resolve from the caller's Company claim.</li>
        <li>Two explicit dimensions that use different actions still combine with AND.</li>
    </ul>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Add an application-specific dimension">
    <p>
        The standard seven markers are conveniences, not a closed list. Define a
        dynamic TypeAuth action in your application's action tree, register that
        tree through your normal <code>AddTypeAuth</code> setup, and use the action
        in a repository declaration:
    </p>

    <DocSnippet Name="DataLevelAccessCustomAction" Language="cs" />
    <DocSnippet Name="DataLevelAccessCustomDimension" Language="cs" />

    <p>
        With no <code>HashId</code> declaration, the action's item IDs must be
        numeric strings that can be parsed to the entity key type. Add
        <code>HashId&lt;YourDto&gt;()</code> when the action stores encoded IDs.
        A custom dimension AND-composes with any other dimensions on that entity.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="How v2 and legacy coexist">
    <p>The repository chooses one effective path for an entity:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><b>No host opt-in and no v2 declaration:</b> existing legacy data-level behavior remains unchanged.</li>
        <li><b>No host opt-in but a v2 declaration exists:</b> the repository fails closed when it tries to use the missing scoped engine.</li>
        <li><b>Host opt-in with an explicit declaration:</b> that v2 declaration is the complete policy for an unmarked entity; the legacy row and query checks do not also run.</li>
        <li><b>Host opt-in with markers:</b> enabled marker dimensions form the v2 policy.</li>
        <li><b>Host opt-in, markers, and an explicit declaration:</b> the declaration replaces standard dimensions for matching actions and extends the rest.</li>
        <li><b>Host opt-in with no marker and no declaration:</b> the standard profile contributes nothing, so the repository keeps its legacy fallback.</li>
        <li><b>Explicit <code>Unscoped()</code>:</b> both standard and legacy data-level checks are deliberately disabled for that entity.</li>
    </ul>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Warning">
        Do not disable a legacy dimension and replace it with a query-only filter.
        That leaves inserts, edits, and deletes without the matching row check.
        A v2 declaration is the replacement because it controls both paths.
    </DocCallout>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Warning">
        If your host replaced <code>IDefaultDataLevelAccess</code> or relies on a
        custom legacy global filter, audit that behavior before opting in. Once
        an effective v2 policy exists, it is the complete query-and-row verdict;
        the legacy implementation does not run beside it.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Read and write behavior">
    <p>The repository selects the TypeAuth access level at each operation stage:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Lists and single-record fetches use <b>Read</b>, including the fetch before an edit or delete.</li>
        <li>An inserted row and the mapped result of an edit use <b>Write</b>.</li>
        <li>The row authorization before a delete uses <b>Delete</b>.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        A Read-only grant can therefore return a row without permitting a later
        update or delete. An empty accessible set denies the dimension. A wildcard
        passes that dimension. Every declared dimension must pass before the
        operation is allowed.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Reuse the policy in a manual query">
    <p>
        Normal repository queries already apply data-level access. If a custom
        query starts directly from a <code>DbSet</code>, reuse the repository's
        effective policy instead of rebuilding the predicate by hand:
    </p>

    <DocSnippet Name="DataLevelAccessManualQuery" Language="cs" />

    <p>
        Inject the scoped <code>DataLevelAccessContext</code> into the repository.
        The public <code>DataLevelAccessPolicy</code> property includes standard
        markers and explicit overrides, and it is resolved lazily after the
        repository constructor has finalized its options.
    </p>

    <DocCallout Type="DocCalloutType.Warning">
        A null v2 policy does not mean that a raw <code>DbSet</code> is safe to
        return. It may mean the repository is using the legacy fallback. Start
        from <code>GetIQueryable</code> in that case, or fail closed as the example
        does.
    </DocCallout>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Choose NotFound or Forbidden for denied row fetches">
    <p>
        A denied single-record Read fetch returns Not Found by default. This avoids
        revealing whether an out-of-scope ID exists. The same fetch happens before
        an edit or delete. An application that prefers an explicit Forbidden response
        for an existing row outside Read scope can declare it with the policy:
    </p>

    <DocSnippet Name="DataLevelAccessForbiddenDisclosure" Language="cs" />

    <p>
        This choice changes single-row fetch disclosure, including the prefetch for
        edit and delete: default NotFound conceals an out-of-Read-scope row as 404,
        while Forbidden surfaces it as 403. Lists remain filtered. If a row is
        Read-visible but the caller lacks Write or Delete for it, the later operation
        row check returns 403 under either disclosure setting.
    </p>
</DocSection>
